In the v4 docs, `@theme` is the directive for **design tokens that also drive utility-generation**.  
`@theme` variables are not just normal CSS variables: they are parsed by Tailwind and used to decide which utility APIs exist (or variants), and they are also exposed as CSS custom properties you can reference via `var(...)` ([`theme.mdx:18-19`, `theme.mdx:56-60`, `theme.mdx:42-49`](/Users/davis/Developer/highmatter/grep-bench/.pi-bench/workspaces/2026-05-03T00-25-17-303Z/repos/tailwindcss/src/docs/theme.mdx)).

### How mapping works
- You define variables in a `@theme` block (top-level) ([`theme.mdx:24-30`]).
- Variables belong to namespaces like `--color-*`, `--spacing-*`, etc.; each namespace maps to a specific utility/variant API ([`theme.mdx:131-133`]).
- A variable’s suffix becomes the utility suffix, so naming determines the class name directly ([`theme.mdx:101`]).
- Example from docs: `--color-mint-500` enables classes like `bg-mint-500`, `text-mint-500`, `fill-mint-500` ([`theme.mdx:20-33`]).

### Namespace → generated utilities (3+ examples)
- `--color-*` → color utilities (`bg-*`, `text-*`, `fill-*`) ([`theme.mdx:147-151`])
- `--font-*` → font-family utilities (`font-*`) ([`theme.mdx:155-159`, `theme.mdx:66-70`, `theme.mdx:83-99`])
- `--spacing-*` → spacing/sizing utilities (`px-*`, `max-h-*`, etc.) ([`theme.mdx:211-215`])
- `--breakpoint-*` → responsive variants (`sm:*`, `3xl:*`) ([`theme.mdx:195-199`, `theme.mdx:103-117`])

### Minimal local example

```css
/* app.css */
@import "tailwindcss";

@theme {
  --color-mint-500: oklch(0.72 0.11 178);
  --font-display: Inter, sans-serif;
  --spacing-18: 4.5rem;
  --breakpoint-3xl: 120rem;
}
```

```html
<!-- example.html -->
<div class="grid grid-cols-2 3xl:grid-cols-6 bg-mint-500 font-display p-18">
  Theme-mapped classes from @theme namespaces
</div>
```

(Here the `3xl` prefix is generated from `--breakpoint-3xl`; `bg-mint-500` from `--color-*`; `font-display` from `--font-*`; and `p-18` from `--spacing-*`.)

